From: draft
Subject: [DRAFT] clamp wrapped bytes_written_ indexing to the last valid uint64 entry

Status: DRAFT, not validated against the real crash. The original offending
offset is unavailable, so this reproduces and fixes the boundary arithmetic but
is not confirmed against the assertion that actually occurred. Building and
real-model validation are required before proposing anything for production or
for this project's patched runtime clone.

Problem
-------
PendingBytes() indexes bytes_written_ with a wrapped command-queue byte offset:

  return bytes_queued_ - bytes_written_[WrapIntoRing(hw_read_index)];

WrapIntoRing(index) = index & (kQueueSize - 1), where kQueueSize = 8 MiB, so a
wrapped offset is always in [0, kQueueSize). bytes_written_ converts that byte
offset to a uint64 data index with:

  uint32_t convert(uint32_t index) { return (index + sizeof(uint64_t) - 1) / sizeof(uint64_t); }

For a wrapped offset in the final partial uint64 region, e.g. offset 8388604,
convert returns ceil(8388604/8) = 1048576 while data_ has exactly
convert(kQueueSize) = 1048576 entries indexed 0..1048575. That indexes one past
the end and reproduces the documented boundary arithmetic for hypothetical
offset 0x7ffffc.

Fix
---
Clamp the converted index to data_.size() - 1 only at the indexing sites
(operator[] and fill). Leave convert and resize unchanged so that
resize(kQueueSize) still produces exactly capacity entries and a non-power-of-two
capacity still gets ceil(size/8) entries. A wrapped offset in the final partial
uint64 belongs to the last entry, so clamping to capacity-1 is correct there and
does not change behavior for any offset outside that region.

--- a/amd_blit_sdma.h
+++ b/amd_blit_sdma.h
@@ class bytes_written_ wrapper:
     uint64_t& operator[](uint32_t index) { return data_[convert(index)]; }
 
     void resize(size_t size) { data_.resize(convert(size)); }
 
     void fill(uint32_t start, uint32_t stop, uint64_t value) {
       for (uint32_t i = convert(start); i < convert(stop); i++) {
         data_[i] = value;
       }
     }
 
    private:
     uint32_t convert(uint32_t index) { return (index + sizeof(uint64_t) - 1) / sizeof(uint64_t); }
 
     std::vector<uint64_t> data_;
   } bytes_written_;
+
+--- proposed change --------------------------------------------------------
+    uint64_t& operator[](uint32_t index) {
+      uint32_t idx = convert(index);
+      if (idx >= data_.size()) idx = data_.size() - 1;
+      return data_[idx];
+    }
+
+    void fill(uint32_t start, uint32_t stop, uint64_t value) {
+      uint32_t begin = convert(start);
+      if (begin >= data_.size()) begin = data_.size() - 1;
+      for (uint32_t i = begin; i < convert(stop); i++) {
+        data_[i] = value;
+      }
+    }
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+Notes and validation plan
+-------------------------
+- convert and resize stay exactly as upstream so capacity sizing is unchanged;
  only wrapped indexing is clamped to the last valid entry.
+- Whether clamping is semantically correct at every wrap boundary should be
  reviewed against the ring fill/operator[] call sites before trusting it; the
  real failing offset is unavailable so this cannot be confirmed against the
  actual assertion.
+- Even if the assertion stops occurring, whether that recovers the measured
  ~10% steady-decode penalty is not established by this draft because the
  document does not prove that the assertion causes that penalty rather than
  being a separate crash.
+- Validation for this project: build a separately-loaded runtime clone and run
  the real-model acceptance gates (exact outputs/full top-20 logprobs, steered
  image continuation, long-generation stability) comparing patched vs unpatched
  before proposing anything for production or for the project-local clone.
+- When upstream ships a confirmed fix, drop this draft and adopt upstream; the
  change is small and isolated to one wrapper so it is easy to remove.
